### Scope

`a_c` governs the form of a symbolic axiomatic model (P2; rc.2 #scope). It
answers:

- what identities and typed objects exist;
- how relations bind exact endpoints;
- how constraints, latitude and residuals remain explicit;
- how traversals and judgments are typed;
- how material closure, projection, overlay and transformation behave;
- what a model must preserve from its subject; and
- which claims are structural, semantic or authority decisions.

`a_c` does not choose the subject vocabulary, decide which source
declarations are material, supply semantic acceptance, select a carrier,
execute a model or prove that one interpretation is uniquely correct (P2,
P6).

### Exclusions

`a_c` is not (P2; rc.2 #exclusions):

- a representation or compression of its derivation source;
- a selected semantic model of any subject;
- a carrier profile or carrier instance;
- an executable language, interpreter, compiler implementation or
  runtime;
- a deterministic semantic assessor or theorem prover;
- a required storage topology of any shape;
- an ontology of any domain;
- an authority registry; or
- a claim that all governed systems share one vocabulary or relation set.

## 10. Conformance obligations

Review and qualification of this member cover at least (P6; rc.2
#conformance-obligations):

1. closed signature, `RecordKind_ac`, population, field, value-domain and
   reference-domain definitions;
2. well-typed positive and refusal examples for every fundamental law;
3. identity uniqueness and cross-context collision cases;
4. closure, projection, overlay, transformation and composition laws,
   including exact model identities, population partitions, basis
   equality, and byte-or-typed-equality preservation refusal;
5. strict separation of domain operations, functor kinds, actors and
   judgments;
6. residual and authority conservation through interpretation and
   encoding;
7. exact calculus-basis identity reconstruction;
8. direct-coordinate, cross-family reference, wrong-family,
   duplicate-family and dangling-reference refusal cases for every
   fundamental record family;
9. derivation-basis and publication-basis reconstruction with cyclic and
   same-carrier counterexamples; and
10. one neutral toy model demonstrating that the calculus has no hidden
    derivation-source record, application-profile or carrier dependency.

No example, checker, model or carrier can qualify a law outside the exact
claim it evaluates (P6; EVALUATION §1).

### Functional realisation checklist

When a project claims functional bias, pure transforms, immutable
carriers, single-owner truth or low-coupling realisation, review
explicitly checks (P6, P5;
rc.2 #14a-functional-realization-review-checklist):

- [ ] the change preserves the constitutional WHAT and keeps realisation
      work inside lawful HOW surfaces only;
- [ ] the active boundary has one accepted Ontology that derives its
      entities, identities, relationships, invariants, lifecycle,
      authority, atomic functions, higher-order composition, effects and
      projections;
- [ ] the entity-lifecycle completeness matrix gives every declare/create,
      read/project, update/transition and delete/retire question a lawful
      function, reasoned `not_applicable` or named owned gap;
- [ ] the authority matrix distinguishes actor, capability, proposal,
      evaluation, verification, admission, execution, projection and
      retirement without implicit authority widening;
- [ ] the function-derivation matrix disposes every discovered
      functionality item and prevents direct verb-to-operation promotion;
- [ ] whole-family Prime contraction has factored repeated behaviour into
      parameterised atomic functions and declared higher-order
      composition before peer functions, operations or carriers are
      promoted;
- [ ] Prime evidence covers the complete candidate semantic-atom family,
      records every accepted atom's admitted domain, and maps accepted
      atoms to every proposed realisation projection;
- [ ] the IACS, three views, public contracts, adapters, implementation
      and tests are fidelity-checked projections of the accepted Ontology
      rather than independent design authorities;
- [ ] if a reference realisation exists, the new realisation is
      functionally equivalent at the semantic boundary without copying
      incidental implementation drift;
- [ ] if a reference realisation exists, the target design explicitly maps
      reference design to target design to target module boundary before
      implementation;
- [ ] before constructing a material generic capability, the boundary ran
      the proportional foundation-selection pass (UP-023, §5), recorded
      bounded discovery and the undominated frontier, distinguished
      declared relations from generic mechanics, preserved every assigned
      role and authority tuple, and recorded decisive evidence for
      selected and rejected candidates;
- [ ] implementation and unit tests both derive from the same module
      boundary assets rather than from code-first decomposition;
- [ ] each semantic truth surface still has one clear authoritative owner;
- [ ] the change reduces duplicated truth, controller reconstruction and
      rival local authority paths;
- [ ] semantic functions consume admitted carriers only;
- [ ] semantic functions are pure for the same admitted inputs;
- [ ] time, randomness, UUID minting, environment reads, process state,
      filesystem, network and global registries are absent from the
      semantic centre;
- [ ] if identity is introduced, identity creation is explicitly owned by
      a declared ingress or effect boundary rather than hidden inside
      semantic helpers;
- [ ] carriers are immutable and returned as new values rather than
      mutated in place;
- [ ] shared mutable state is absent from the semantic centre;
- [ ] semantic transforms operate directly on local admitted carriers
      rather than re-entering parsers, validators or loaders on locally
      assembled truth;
- [ ] invalid or incomplete truth is rejected at ingress instead of being
      repaired procedurally inside semantic code;
- [ ] defaults are explicit, ratified and carrier-owned rather than
      helper-owned;
- [ ] each function has one clear owner and one clear responsibility rather
      than mixing admission, semantics and effects;
- [ ] coupling is low by dependency shape: semantic algebra depends on
      carrier contracts, not runtime shells; admission depends on
      validation, not semantic orchestration; effect code stays at the
      edge;
- [ ] no function both decides semantic meaning and performs effects;
- [ ] effect boundaries are explicit, typed and unable to erase carrier
      truth into open objects;
- [ ] package, bootstrap, controller, adapter and runtime-shell layers stay
      below the semantic centre;
- [ ] governance and observability surfaces publish facts, eligibility and
      provenance only, without imperative builder or executor strategy;
- [ ] the active boundary has complete Mermaid domain, sequence and
      state-machine views that match the current IACS, visibility,
      ownership, behaviour, lifecycle and deferred-family split;
- [ ] the active boundary carries a complete cross-view axiom matrix and
      an explicit `accepted` design verdict with no bypassed realisation
      gap;
- [ ] the unit tests for the active boundary derive from module ownership,
      IACS and structural carrier assets rather than from helper layout;
- [ ] if the change includes clean-up or optimisation, it stays
      boundary-local, behaviour-preserving and authority-neutral;
- [ ] if the same realisation pattern has appeared before, the ticket has
      made an explicit library or commonisation decision rather than
      rebuilding it again silently;
- [ ] if a reusable library or commonisation surface exists, the ticket
      explicitly consumes or extends it rather than duplicating the
      pattern locally;
- [ ] if no reusable library or commonisation surface is used, there is an
      explicit rationale that the pattern is boundary-specific or not yet
      a lawful commonisation candidate;
- [ ] if a cross-boundary opportunity was discovered, it was kept out of
      the current change unless the owning authority admitted it at the
      lawful re-entry point;
- [ ] if the ticket is making a design-method closure claim, the bounded
      design check covered the exact claim and applicable hard stops
      without automatically absorbing optional discovery;
- [ ] a negative proof shows that an imperative bypass, open-payload
      bypass or effect-edge erasure fails closed;
- [ ] if the project is governed by the graph-execution profile, the
      deterministic module sits inside a declared outcome traversal rather
      than replacing one;
- [ ] if the module appears to decide next work, traversal closure,
      semantic target movement or graph-function identity, the boundary
      has been repriced under the graph-execution profile;
- [ ] the active boundary carries lifecycle confirmation against the
      canonical operational lifecycle chain of SPEC_METHOD §2, with each
      phase answered, declared not applicable with a reason, or recorded
      as a named `Gap:` or `Unanswered:` item; and
- [ ] release, deployment, live usage, telemetry and retirement claims are
      confirmed against product, requirement, design, release or
      operational authority rather than derived from implementation
      convention or fixtures.

For a new or materially changed boundary, the compact hard-gate version of
this checklist is (P6):

- [ ] accepted Ontology before promotion or closure and, where
      design-gated, before retained implementation establishes an
      unresolved or contradictory relation or chooses a materially
      non-equivalent network;
- [ ] complete entity lifecycle, authority and function-derivation
      evidence;
- [ ] whole-family Prime contraction, accepted atom domains, realisation
      projection mappings and declared composition and effect algebra;
- [ ] proportional capability and foundation selection for material
      generic mechanics and dependencies, with bounded discovery, frontier
      disposition, and exact role and authority conservation;
- [ ] pure functions in the semantic centre;
- [ ] immutable carriers with no shared mutable semantic state;
- [ ] one owner per truth surface;
- [ ] low coupling between admission, algebra and effect edges;
- [ ] no hidden runtime authority in semantic code;
- [ ] accepted domain, sequence and state-machine projections of the
      Ontology;
- [ ] complete cross-view axiom evaluation with no bypassed realisation
      gap;
- [ ] functional equivalence to specification and, when present, the
      reference realisation at the semantic boundary;
- [ ] lifecycle confirmation or named lifecycle gaps against the canonical
      operational lifecycle chain of SPEC_METHOD §2; and
- [ ] negative proof for imperative or open bypass.

The checklist is language-agnostic. It governs module design, carrier
design, data-entity design and review of realisation work in any language
(P1). For an unchanged boundary, the hard gate is the accepted basis plus
evidence of no material semantic-boundary delta; it does not require a new
Ontology, IACS, three-view asset, cross-view evaluation or unit-test lane
(P2; §7 Gate and retrospective work).

### 5B. Candidate requires independent review

When independent review is required, the Executive activates the Reviewer
with (P6, P3; rc.2 #5b-candidate-requires-independent-review):

- the exact candidate and governing basis;
- the exact claim, composition boundary, population, scope and exclusions;
- the applicable method and project authority;
- the required evidence and the ordinary assurance path;
- the required user-acceptance, end-to-end, integration, unit,
  combinatorial and negative-path frame results, with their exact claims and
  populations;
- the Product-selected severity scale and the exact affected Product claims;
- the Reviewer's capability and independence envelope; and
- invalidation and stop conditions.

Where EVALUATION §7 requires it, the candidate is frozen before material
reference exposure. Otherwise the subject is stabilised to the exactness
that EVALUATION §3, ACTIVATION §7 and the claim require (P3, P6).

### Adopt this baseline

A baseline-adoption projection records inside that Product-owned basis
(P6, P3; rc.2 #adopt-this-baseline):

- the exact selected complete method, REFERENCE_FRAME and profile
  identities;
- the existing authority admitting adoption;
- the governed workspace, Product scope, outcome, basis and time;
- local Executive, Worker and Reviewer capability envelopes and
  actor-binding rules;
- generic family declarations or authoritative acquisition paths, with an
  authority-bound non-material or explicit residual disposition for each of
  the twelve families of §11;
- specialist-frame declarations or authoritative acquisition paths;
- where testing evidence is material: testing-frame declarations, exact
  Product and module claims, ordinary-path identities, combinatorial
  populations, the scenario matrix, oracles and the forbidden-path
  inventory (ASSURANCE §7 to §9);
- local subject, evidence, checkpoint and review-result coordinates;
- the coverage ledger and the accepted residual boundary; and
- invalidation and frame-set revision conditions.

### Define a project configuration

A project may define different frames, names, overlaps, sequences and actor
bindings. The defining Product, requirement, Goals or accepted-design
authority records (P6, P2; rc.2 #define-a-project-configuration;
REFERENCE_FRAME §4):

- the exact method and project basis;
- the known evaluation inventory;
- each frame declaration and capability envelope;
- semantic, evaluation, operation where material, and decision authority;
- intentional overlap, separately activated evaluations and required
  assurance independence;
- the conjunction, translation and composition actually used;
- the explanation of the allocation, with its material resource limits and
  failure assumptions, that ACTIVATION §1 Configuration requires of every
  configuration;
- the mapping to the applicable construction, assessment, disposition,
  identity and release obligations;
- coverage and residual uncertainty; and
- re-entry, invalidation and revision conditions.

A project configuration need not keep the labels Executive, Worker and
Reviewer or map one local frame to each. It keeps every applicable method
relation and identifies how its evaluations cover the claims it makes. It
is evaluated against its task, its dependencies, its actors' limits, the
authority it applies and the required assurance, as ACTIVATION §1
Configuration states, never by its labels, its topology or its difference
from this profile. A local axioms document may carry the accepted
configuration; its authority is the cited Goals, Product, requirement or
accepted-design decision, not the document's category (P6, P1).

## 21. Anti-patterns

The profile is misapplied when (P6, P2; rc.2 #anti-patterns):

1. Executive, Worker or Reviewer is treated as a persona without a declared
   evaluation and authority relation;
2. the frames are treated as an organisational hierarchy;
3. the Executive is made a universal context, semantic interpreter or
   implementation controller;
4. the Worker sends a candidate directly into an implied Reviewer
   activation;
5. an activation refusal is relabelled as a Worker result, or a work-result
   variant is selected without its required core result;
6. refused, incomplete or re-entry work results have no route to the
   Executive;
7. the Worker accepts its own result, infers continuation, or treats its
   role label or broad workspace access as mutation authority;
8. the Reviewer relies on the Worker's narrative instead of acquiring the
   material live subject;
9. the Reviewer trusts tests without reviewing their semantics where they
   can change the proof;
10. the Reviewer edits or directs repair while keeping independent-review
    status;
11. a changed candidate or basis reuses the predecessor verdict;
12. results from different coordinates are flattened into one relation;
13. a local axioms file is treated as authority without an owning Goals,
    Product, requirement or design decision;
14. this profile restates the execution relation (ACTIVATION §1 and §3),
    independent assessment (EVALUATION §3) or the assurance-boundary law
    (EVALUATION §1 and §7) as peer constitutional law;
15. a project-defined configuration silently changes selected method law;
16. consumer-specific realisation machinery, or a renamed architectural
    equivalent, is presented as a universal requirement;
17. test labels or runner choice stand in for declaring the exact testing
    frame, subject, claim, population, oracle, path and falsification
    condition;
18. unit-test, assertion, snapshot, line, branch or function count is used
    as a Product-confidence or closure measure;
19. implementation-shaped unit tests are treated as independent design,
    architecture, integration, installed or user-outcome assurance;
20. interface, persistence, orchestration, authority, configuration or user
    complexity is mocked away and then counted as unit coverage;
21. helper-level tests become the canonical proof surface for a
    module-owned algebra or for a broader Product composition;
22. an expected output is accepted without proving the required
    authoritative path, or while a material rival path stays reachable;
23. one sunny end-to-end path stands in for a declared combinatorial
    population, an interaction-strength justification, failure coverage and
    a residual set;
24. UAT gains capabilities, information, setup paths or interfaces
    unavailable to the declared user;
25. a harness, fixture, proof script or expected-value builder
    reconstructs owner-issued runtime meaning and treats the reconstruction
    as the assurance subject;
26. a downstream symptom is assigned to one owner when the evidence
    establishes a joint causal frontier, unresolved alternatives or no
    bounded frontier, or localisation is used to authorise a wider repair;
27. a mutation, sabotage, fixture or proof capture changes the exact
    assurance subject without isolation, restoration and revalidation;
28. aggregate green status hides a missing or conflicting user-acceptance,
    end-to-end, integration, unit or negative-path result;
29. a generic family is treated as a required component, actor, service,
    file, runtime type, stage or new semantic owner;
30. a material generic family is omitted because no artifact shares its
    name, or several families are flattened into one broad frame without
    preserving their distinct evaluation and result relations;
31. the Reviewer assigns priority, decides promotion blocking, directs
    repair or starts another activation; or
32. the Executive collapses severity, priority, frame result,
    promotion-boundary effect and disposition into one status value.

### ABG

ABG owns traversal; dispatch; actor invocation; process supervision;
graph-call realisation; runtime facts; runtime event emission and replay;
frames; continuations; retry and correction control; closure folds over
runtime truth; re-entry after publish boundaries; lineage and provenance
(P6).

## 16. Failure pattern

This profile is violated when any of these holds (P6):

1. a claimed product step has no corresponding node or vector;
2. a graph function exists with no clear outer contract;
3. automation performs work that cannot be described as a manual
   traversal;
4. the installed product depends on authoring-carrier knowledge that a
   clean target project should not contain;
5. scenarios cannot name the significant paths they intend to prove;
6. the project claims closure without target-relative evidence;
7. "last-output feeds next-input" wiring bypasses the cumulative
   environment law;
8. zoomed and unzoomed views disagree about whether a carrier is closed;
9. imperative service code owns an operative transition that has no
   published graph-function outcome contract;
10. framework behaviour is implemented as workspace orchestration first
    and described with GTL vocabulary only later;
11. imperative code decides closure, continuation, semantic target
    movement or lineage truth outside declared graph-function, ABG runtime
    or product-owned carrier truth;
12. a GTL/ABG product or plugin carries hidden execution authority for
    traversal loops, actor invocation, process supervision, retry,
    continuation, runtime events, projection or closure folds;
13. yield or lawful iteration is flattened into retry, block, timeout, a
    local waiting state or a hidden CLI loop instead of being represented
    as a replay-visible closure disposition;
14. a public query, gaps view, postflight string, service method or
    installed operator summary can decide next work outside the
    constructive evaluation loop;
15. a constitutional process cannot be followed end to end by an agentic
    coder from the declared authority, or a produced constitutional
    artifact is accepted without passing deterministic admission
    (§11.7A).

## 17. Invocation rule

When an agent or designer is told `using ODD, build this product`, the
expected sequence is (P2, P0):

1. read SPEC_METHOD and keep the constitutional chain intact;
2. read the product-specific method if one exists;
3. read the local product authority surfaces: `INTENT.md`, `PRODUCT.md`,
   the relevant requirement families and the active design surfaces;
4. identify the product's semantic chain from source to published outcome;
5. identify the domain-specific typed assets or nodes;
6. identify the public graph-function carriers;
7. define the machine-readable function catalogue;
8. define or update the GTL module, including the outer callable carriers
   and the live traversal boundaries;
9. define how coarse carriers, refined inner carriers and executable proof
   lanes close together without scale contradiction;
10. complete the execution-authority audit for the affected runtime,
    plugin, worker, event, projection, assurance, closure or continuation
    surfaces;
11. define the constructive evaluation loop from intent lineage and model
    synthesis through observation, target binding, lawful action
    selection, admitted evidence, fulfilment ledger, closure decision and
    next-action projection;
12. define the closure decision vocabulary, including `yield` as lawful
    iteration and not retry, block, timeout or a local waiting state;
13. define the projection and query lane as a projection over admitted
    constructive history, including the structural coherence rule between
    the read model and its source carrier;
14. only then implement code, policy and transport surfaces.

The instruction does not mean starting from imperative scripts and adding
GTL words later, treating graph functions as documentation wrappers, or
letting service code become the hidden real carrier (P0, P6).

### General ODD product

1. SPEC_METHOD;
2. this profile;
3. the product's `stdo_<label>.json` definition;
4. the applicable bound project reference-frame basis and the authorised
   agent activation packet;
5. the bound product Intent;
6. the bound Product definition;
7. the relevant bound product requirements;
8. the active bound design surfaces;
9. the GTL module, function catalogue and query surfaces.

### World-model product

1. SPEC_METHOD;
2. this profile;
3. the world-model profile (WORLD_MODEL);
4. the product's `stdo_<label>.json` definition;
5. the applicable bound project reference-frame basis and the authorised
   agent activation packet;
6. the bound product Intent;
7. the bound Product definition;
8. the relevant bound requirement families;
9. the current bound design surfaces;
10. the GTL module, function catalogue, query surfaces and proof lane.

In practice this profile sets the structural expectation, the
product-specific method sets the domain law, and the local product
surfaces set the current build line. An LLM that skips the order will
usually either understand the paradigm but miss the domain, or understand
the domain but realise it through the wrong carrier (P1, P2).

## 1. Position

Posting is the project's commentary layer. Posts are for (P6; rc.2
#posting-guide):

- analysis;
- criticism;
- proposed changes;
- handoff; and
- repricing of confidence.

A post is not constitutional truth. It is working commentary, and it may
later influence specification or design (P6).

## 5. Categories

A post takes one of these categories (P3; rc.2 #categories):

- `REVIEW`: critique of code, specification, design or another post;
- `STRATEGY`: a proposed technical or methodological direction;
- `GAP`: a precise insufficiency or contradiction;
- `SCHEMA`: the shape of a data model, event model, interface or contract;
- `HANDOFF`: a bounded transfer of context or task state; and
- `MATRIX`: an explicit comparison of options for a decision.

## 6. Structure

A post uses this base shape (P3, P6; rc.2 #structure):

```markdown
# {CATEGORY}: {Subject}

**Author**: {agent}
**Date**: {ISO 8601}
**Addresses**: {artifact, issue, or boundary under discussion}
**Status**: Draft
**Updated**: {ISO 8601, optional}

## Summary
{short summary}

## Analysis
{main content}

## Recommended Action
{next step}
```

In a `MATRIX` post the central section is a decision table rather than
free prose (P3).

## 4. Frame-set declaration

Human authority may declare an initial project frame set from accumulated
development practice, known failure modes, domain expertise, the governed
outcome's risk and knowledge of the available agents and other actors. The
declaration need not reconstruct the set from the workspace bottom up (P6,
P2; rc.2 #human-frame-set-declaration). It records:

- the governed workspace and outcome;
- the known evaluation inventory;
- the selected frames and their capability envelopes;
- intentional overlaps and separately activated checks;
- known frame dependencies or translations;
- semantic, evaluation, operation where material, decision and acceptance
  authority;
- known exclusions and residual uncertainty; and
- the conditions for revision.

Human selection is a lawful source of method structure. It proves neither
that the selected set is complete nor that the frames hold authority over
the subject relations they inspect (P6; RF-006, RF-015).

## 7. Frame relations

Frames may relate as (rc.2 #frame-relations):

- disjoint: no material variable, coordinate, evidence or result dependency
  is shared for the declared evaluations;
- overlapping: some material variables, evidence or entities are shared;
- complementary: distinct evaluations jointly inform one decision;
- alternative: different frames can evaluate the same claim under declared
  capability or evidence conditions;
- refinement: one frame resolves a material distinction left aggregated in
  another;
- restriction: one frame evaluates a lawful subset of another frame's
  domain;
- translated: values or evidence move through a declared coordinate map;
- temporally successive: a later frame evaluates a changed basis;
- composed: several frame relations form one governed evaluation or
  action; or
- governing and subordinate: one frame inherits declared constraints from
  another where a real authority or decomposition relation exists.

No relation is the default universal frame topology. A project declares
only the relations its evaluations and coverage claims need (P3, P2;
RF-009).

### Operational full coverage

A frame set has operational full coverage for its declared tuple when
(rc.2 #operational-full-coverage):

1. every known material evaluation is covered by at least one capable
   frame;
2. every material interaction has a declared evaluation, conjunction,
   translation or composition relation;
3. every evaluation has semantic and decision authority, and every
   material operation has its inherited operation authority;
4. every mandatory claim has an admissible evidence path and a refusal
   path;
5. the required independently activated overlaps and
   assurance-independence conditions are present;
6. no unresolved frame-result conflict is hidden by aggregation;
7. excluded evaluations cite their governing authority; and
8. uncovered and unknown residuals are explicit and compatible with the
   claim.

Operational full coverage is relative to its tuple. It is not a claim that
no undiscovered evaluation exists (P6; P0 with the open-world premise).

### Missing-frame signals

These observations trigger frame diagnosis (P4b, P5; rc.2
#missing-frame-signals):

- a competent actor repeatedly cannot see a known material violation;
- a local repair creates or duplicates another authority path;
- work repeatedly resets after apparently valid local completion;
- long implementation loops produce no stable closure;
- proof passes while the claimed outcome stays unbound;
- a material failure is visible only after broad integration;
- reviewers repeatedly expand context without reaching a decidable
  question;
- two actors use the same frame name but evaluate different material
  relations; or
- a topology mutation leaves prior verdicts apparently current.

## 11. The activation packet

An actor receives one activated frame or one declared active frame
configuration, never an implied demand to reconstruct the complete
workspace from ambient context (P0; rc.2 #agentic-use). The minimum
activation packet contains:

- frame identity and revision;
- the evaluation and its result algebra;
- the exact subject and basis;
- the material relations and applicable invariants;
- semantic, evaluation, operation where material, and decision authority;
- admissible evidence and tools;
- the capability and context envelope;
- exclusions and prohibited assumptions;
- declared frame dependencies or conjunction outputs; and
- invalidation, stop and reconfiguration conditions.

The actor may own its internal reasoning and search where the governing
method permits. It returns through the declared result relation. Hidden
internal reasoning is no substitute for required evidence, provenance or
authority (P6). Frame size is optimised for reliable evaluation, not for
maximum context loading: a more capable actor may activate a broader frame
and a less capable one may use a lawful refinement sequence, and both
preserve the same material invariants and authority relations (P0, P2;
RF-014).

## 12. Violations

Each of the following is a violation of this member where it supports a
frame or coverage claim (rc.2 #anti-patterns):

1. one omniscient frame is presumed to contain the complete method or
   workspace (P0);
2. a module, file, service, role, persona, prompt or lens is called a
   frame without a declared evaluation relation (RF-001);
3. functional decomposition, authority hierarchy and frame topology are
   silently treated as one tree (RF-009);
4. every active frame is required to compose with every other frame
   (RF-010);
5. composition machinery is introduced without a material cross-frame
   evaluation (RF-010);
6. frame selection or broad visibility is treated as semantic authority
   (RF-006);
7. evidence is flattened across bases or coordinates and treated as one
   executed or observed relation (RF-005, RF-008);
8. `satisfied` is returned where the lawful result is `indeterminate`,
   `out_of_frame` or `invalid_basis` (RF-012);
9. coverage is inferred from artifact, file, operation, test, review or
   actor count (§8);
10. a frame silently grows beyond the selected actor's capability envelope
    (RF-002, RF-014);
11. a topology mutation reuses a stale activation without recharting
    (RF-011);
12. every failure creates another frame before activation, basis,
    evidence, capability and evaluation defects are excluded (§10);
13. intentional overlap is contracted although it supplies independently
    activated failure detection or materially different capability fit
    (RF-017); or
14. a generic runtime, registry, controller or universal carrier is
    presented as required by this member (RF-016).

## 14. Closure

A project operates under this member when (rc.2 #closure):

1. human authority has declared the governed workspace, outcome,
   evaluation inventory and frame set (§4);
2. every active frame is finite, evaluation-bound, basis-bound,
   authority-safe and usable inside its actor capability envelope (§3);
3. frame results preserve semantic, evidence, inference and decision
   distinctions (RF-007);
4. cross-frame conjunction, translation or composition exists only where a
   material evaluation requires it (§6);
5. workspace coverage maps every known material evaluation and interaction
   to a capable frame or an explicit residual (§8);
6. operational full coverage is stated relative to its exact tuple (§8);
7. topology, capability, evidence and historical failures can trigger
   frame revision or recharting (§10); and
8. no executable frame machinery, global context, universal completeness
   or domain authority is inferred from adopting this member (RF-016, §9).

## 2. Release identities

The release-publication context distinguishes (P3;
rc.2 #release-identities):

- Project Release Namespace: a stable repository-local key assigned by
  the Product release authority to one independently released source
  project;
- Project Subtree: the exact repository-relative source-project root and
  Git tree object selected for one cut;
- Candidate Source: the candidate source of FOUNDATIONS §2, the mutable
  source-project state constructing a possible successor;
- RC Branch: a mutable project-qualified carrier for the current
  candidate window;
- Immutable RC Cut: an annotated project-qualified Git tag plus its exact
  Product release subject (FOUNDATIONS §2), naming one published candidate
  and, once accepted, one released Product; it realises the release cut;
- Version-Line Selector: the mutable project-qualified tag alias naming
  the highest-ordinal published immutable RC cut for that project and
  version line; it realises the version-line selector of FOUNDATIONS §2;
  and
- Release Branch: an optional mutable project-qualified convenience
  carrier aligned to the same latest-published commit as the selector.

Equal version text does not collapse these identities. Branches and the
version-line selector may move. An immutable RC tag never moves (P3).

### Project-subtree release identity

In a shared release source, an immutable cut binds all of (P3;
rc.2 #project-subtree-release-identity):

- the Project Release Namespace and owning Product authority;
- the exact qualified Git ref and annotated tag object;
- the peeled commit and repository-root tree;
- the normalised repository-relative Project Subtree root at that commit;
- the Git tree object at that exact Project Subtree root;
- the declared Product member inventory and release-scoped claim bytes;
  and
- the predecessor and successor dispositions required by §9.

The Project Subtree root is a locator inside the exact peeled commit, not
Product identity. Its canonical spelling is the slash-separated Git path
from the repository root, with no leading or trailing slash and no empty,
`.` or `..` component; `.` alone denotes the repository root. Each
non-root component names the exact tree entry bytes traversed at that
commit. Moving a source project changes this locator and its cut identity
without reassigning the Project Release Namespace. If the source project
occupies the repository root, its project-subtree tree equals the
repository-root tree (P3).

A tag over a monorepo commit does not make sibling subtrees Product
members. Unrelated sibling bytes remain carrier state outside the declared
Product subject. Conversely, a member inventory or payload digest without
the qualified ref, commit, Project Subtree root and Project Subtree tree is
not a complete reacquirable release identity (P3, P6).

### Mutable candidate construction

During candidate construction (P0, P6;
rc.2 #mutable-candidate-construction):

- bounded fixes are allowed after intake triage;
- qualification and operator review may continue;
- release notes may remain draft until RC publication;
- the RC branch may move; and
- mutable source is explicitly non-operative for released consumers.

The intended Product boundary, release-scoped claims, excluded
source-project state and qualification basis become explicit before RC
publication. A material scope change is repriced instead of being silently
absorbed (P2, P6).

### Immutable RC publication

A published RC cut exists to provide one exact subject for review, replay
and possible acceptance. To publish it, the project (P3, P5, P6;
rc.2 #immutable-rc-publication):

1. confirms the triaged scope and lawful constitutional re-entry;
2. declares the exact Product release subject and release-scoped claim
   set;
3. reconciles the release note and other release-facing assets to that
   subject;
4. runs the proportionate pre-RC qualification and operator checks;
5. commits the exact carrier;
6. resolves and records the exact Project Release Namespace, Project
   Subtree root and Project Subtree tree;
7. creates one annotated immutable `<project>/v<version>-rc.<n>` tag and
   an annotated `<project>/v<version>` selector tag over that same commit
   in a shared release source;
8. atomically pushes the carrier, RC branch, immutable RC tag, selector
   and optional release branch where the transport supports it;
9. verifies remotely that the immutable tag, selector and release branch
   resolve to the highest published RC commit; and
10. records enough ref, subtree, identity and inventory evidence to
    reacquire the cut without trusting a mutable checkout.

Each published RC tag is immutable. Further work increments `<n>` on the
same version line when the intended line remains applicable. It does not
require a minor-version increment merely because review found a bounded
repair. A published higher RC without matching selector advancement is an
invalid, fail-closed release state; it never authorises resolution of the
older cut as the version-line latest (P3, P5).

### Break order and break-to-closure map

An implementation migration ticket that touches a core interface includes
a `Required Break Order` or an equivalent ordered execution section. The
order starts at the authoritative source carrier and moves outward
(P4b, P6; rc.2 #required-break-order-and-break-to-closure-map;
[SPEC_METHOD §7](SPEC_METHOD.md#hard-break-discipline)):

1. discover the full best-guess interface family;
2. publish or admit the new source carrier;
3. deliberately sever one old authoritative seam and keep it broken;
4. rebind the deepest semantic kernel to that carrier;
5. remove or demote old producers;
6. rebind old consumers;
7. reprice projections, reports, prompts, delivery bindings and tests.

When the migration has several old interfaces, the ticket also includes a
`Break-To-Closure Map` stating which break closes which closure-law clause;
without it a partial landing can look like a full landing (P6).

Reviewers treat these as explicit defects: a typed wrapper that still
carries old semantic authority; a new carrier added beside the old
authoritative read model; a proxy or partial implementation that keeps the
old interface alive behind a new name; constructor, replay, ingest, resume,
bootstrap or public-wrapper paths that bypass admission; a projection or
test that can pass without the new carrier; a fallback path that invents
missing policy, identity, target or closure truth; a downstream proof that
passes while upstream source-carrier work remains open (P6, P3).

## Scope

The profile represents (P3, P4b):

- one immutable occurrence for one exact traversal application;
- a stable binding to an external subject whose reality may change;
- bounded pre-effect and post-effect observations;
- intended typed outcomes;
- separately authorised effect operations and their evidence;
- immutable admitted framework-event facts;
- candidate claims, judgments and materialised typed relations;
- explicit event frontiers and projections bound to a frontier; and
- distinct identity dependency, causal subgraphs and wider typed lineage.

It defines no process scheduler, event-log carrier, state store, workspace
format, rollback mechanism, actor topology or prospective reference-frame
profile (P6, P0).

## Exclusions

This profile is not (P2, P6):

- a change to pure `a_c` or to pure reference-frame law;
- a mandatory profile for every symbolic model;
- a concrete subject or workspace ontology;
- a process, traversal, retry, continuation or closure engine;
- an event store, event-log schema, projection implementation or state
  carrier;
- a mutation, admission, evaluation or decision authority;
- a prospective-frame or sensor-population profile;
- a guarantee that every framework event belongs to an occurrence; or
- a claim that every typed lineage graph is acyclic.

## Conformance obligations

Review and qualification cover at least (P6, P5):

1. instantiation under one exact `a_c` basis, the exact eight-member
   `RecordKind_ac` population function, and closed reference domains
   without shadowing an inherited field;
2. exact inherited record families, direct coordinates, and disjoint
   local and external identity populations;
3. complete occurrence seeds, exact congruence with every mirroring
   relation, and refusal of future results and future events;
4. first-event admission without a fictional predecessor;
5. distinct candidate, judgment, event, materialised relation, cut and
   cut-judgment identities with the unchanged subject's digest bound;
6. idempotent or refused duplicate admission;
7. cycles in each identity or causal subgraph;
8. lawful opposing support and correction edges in wider typed lineage;
9. the exact closed event-kind population, payload, digest, scope and
   binding law, including scope-independent events with no fabricated
   `event_for` relation;
10. separation of functor kind, traversal, operation kind, operation
    instance, executor, actor, grant, invocation, target and territory;
11. missing, stale, mismatched and out-of-territory operation grants;
12. no-effect, complete-effect, partial-effect and refused-effect cases;
13. preservation of the external mutable subject as the reality carrier;
14. fresh occurrence identity on traversal reapplication;
15. component and aggregate occurrence identities for retained composite
    applications;
16. refusal on a stale pre-effect observation and on grant currentness;
17. refusal of an incomplete semantic-admission batch, including a
    missing or non-admitted cut judgment and unequal claim, event and cut
    source frontiers;
18. frontier completeness, staleness and correction selection;
19. non-causation by timestamp, ordinal, adjacency, containment and
    correlation;
20. absent Product adoption and refusal of an incomplete mapping; and
21. profile-basis reconstruction from exact JCS, kinds, schemas,
    immutable URIs, digests, the calculus address and unique publication
    membership.

Representative examples or checkers establish only the claims they
actually evaluate. They do not become an occurrence engine or a semantic
authority (P6).

## 3. Product truth and admitted carriers

UX surfaces are projection and control surfaces: they render admitted
truth and emit typed messages (P6, P0;
rc.2 #3a-ux-projection-and-constructive-carrier-boundary). A UX-local Msg
may govern view-local concerns such as expansion state, sort order,
filter text, focus movement or draft input. A Msg that changes product
truth is not merely a UX concern. Product-truth changes include:

- writing, creating, moving or deleting assets;
- changing Context, selection authority, pinning or global product state;
- spawning, attaching or resuming sessions;
- dispatching agents, jobs, workorders, graph calls or runtime commands;
- changing closure, approval, continuation, lineage, provenance or
  evidence;
- publishing, accepting, rejecting or superseding product records.

Such a message maps to one admitted product carrier (P6, P4a):

- an AssetSurface action;
- a published graph function or lawful graph-function composition;
- a workorder, job or runtime command admitted by the product's design;
- an ABG event or command boundary where ABG is the runtime authority;
- another typed product contract named by the design module.

The UX layer does not become the hidden constructive carrier. If no
admitted carrier exists for a product-truth-changing message, the UX
surface is blocked until the carrier is authored or the requirement is
repriced (P6, P5; §15 states the result).

## 5. State transitions

State changes through typed messages applied by the pure reducer, the
Update of §4 (P4a, P1; rc.2 #5-state-transition-law). A UX surface
declares:

- its State shape;
- its Msg algebra;
- its `Update : (Msg, State) → (State, Cmd)`;
- its Cmd algebra;
- its Sub algebra when the surface subscribes to external or runtime
  events.

Each Cmd declaration names (P5, P4b):

- the effect kind;
- the typed input payload;
- the admitted carrier or external boundary it targets;
- the success Msg emitted back to the reducer;
- the failure Msg emitted back to the reducer;
- any deterministic correlation key required for replay.

The reducer is pure: it does not perform I/O, log to side channels or
mutate global state. Effects scheduled by Cmd are interpreted by the
runtime at the effect membrane (§6) (P0, P1).

View-local ephemera (open or closed flags, hover state, draft input) may
be held in implementation-local state cells where the framework provides
them, but only when the value is genuinely view-local: not observed by
other views, not part of the product's state surface, and not
consequential after the view unmounts. Product-meaningful state goes
through the reducer without exception (P6, P3). The discipline test for
whether state belongs in the reducer or in view-local ephemera: if losing
the value on unmount changes the product's behaviour or another part of
the UI's view, it belongs in the reducer (P3).

## 12. Adoption

Product authority adopts this profile for a project (§1 Authority), and
the adoption is recorded by (P6, P4b; rc.2 #13-adoption-guidance):

1. naming the profile in the project's UX-related design modules;
2. recording the realisation stack choice (§4 Realisation discretion) and
   the justification;
3. declaring the irreducible architectural carrier set and the structural
   carrier diagram the design profile requires (DESIGN_MODULE §6 and §7);
4. declaring the State, Msg, Update, Cmd and Sub shapes for each UX
   surface in the design module before implementation;
5. declaring the AssetSurface or equivalent typed-contract bindings (§7)
   the surface consumes and emits to;
6. naming the effect-membrane mechanism (§6) and the Cmd algebra it
   interprets;
7. naming the executable Msg-replay proof surface required by §8;
8. naming any §11 accessibility refinement the project requires;
9. noting any §9 view-shape or §6 effect-membrane exemptions explicitly.

Adoption is per project. A project may adopt this profile only for some
UX surfaces, for example the production UI but not internal debug tools,
with the scope recorded in the design module (P2, P6).

### Scaffold and exemption

A UX surface is governed by this profile by default when any of these are
true (P6, P5; rc.2 #13a-scaffold-and-exemption-law):

- it is reachable from the product shell;
- it is used as proof for a ticket, requirement, design module, release or
  review;
- it can mutate product truth;
- it can spawn, attach, resume or dispatch work;
- it can change Context, selection authority, closure, continuation,
  evidence or provenance.

A scaffold, prototype, debug pane or temporary proof surface may be
exempt only when the design module or ticket records (P6, P4b):

- the exemption scope;
- the permitted operations;
- whether mutation is forbidden or explicitly bounded;
- a visible scaffold or debug label where user-facing;
- the authoritative surface that will supersede it;
- the retirement condition and owning ticket;
- the non-closure condition preventing release if the scaffold remains.

An exempt scaffold cannot become the production UX by accumulation. It is
retired, repriced into a governed UX surface, or kept outside product
closure (P4b, P6).

## 13. Failure patterns

A UX surface violates this profile when any of these are true (P6, P5;
rc.2 #14-failure-pattern):

1. render code mutates state directly (assignments inside the render
   body, mutating a prop or context value);
2. view-local state cells carry product-meaningful state instead of
   view-local ephemera;
3. an effect handler contains conditional logic that decides state
   transitions;
4. multi-step state machines live in the view rather than in the reducer;
5. mutable refs are read during render outside the declared effect
   membrane;
6. UX-local types shadow or re-declare back-end types instead of
   importing from the shared contract;
7. selection or action emission writes to file or network without going
   through an AssetSurface action;
8. view-shape exemptions (§9) are taken without a recorded design-module
   entry;
9. accessibility is omitted on a public product capability without a
   recorded exemption;
10. two-way binding emits no typed Msg action and the reducer is
    bypassed;
11. logic that should be a Cmd is implemented imperatively inside an
    effect handler;
12. the view's behaviour cannot be replayed by replaying its Msg log
    against an empty State;
13. a product-truth-changing UX message has no admitted carrier, graph
    function, runtime command or typed product contract;
14. external data crosses into UX state without runtime validation;
15. a scaffold or debug surface is reachable, mutating or used as proof
    without the exemption record of §12 Scaffold and exemption;
16. a project-local ADR, component convention or existing code pattern is
    used to weaken this profile;
17. a UX sprint claims closure while deferred proof, accessibility,
    carrier, scaffold or authority debt remains unclassified or
    un-ticketed.

Any of these is a violation of this profile. Repeated violations are not
normalised by usage; they are repaired or the surface is repriced (P6,
P4b).

## 1. Position

Most institutional systems preserve values and lose the semantic context
needed to interpret them. World-model work builds a governed semantic
layer over authoritative source systems, which enact bounded domain
function through code paths, events, records, APIs, schedules,
configurations, documents and downstream obligations (P1, P6;
rc.2 #position). The profile starts from these claims:

- source systems enact domain function and remain sovereign for
  operational truth;
- domains are defined by bounded function and authority;
- records are intelligible only through function, provenance and
  treatment context;
- mappings are semantic treatments over governed meaning;
- catalogs and schemas are explanatory projections over deeper semantic
  structure;
- the world model traces authoritative source surfaces, records accepted
  semantic claims in ledgers, and publishes bounded semantic cuts;
- the published semantic layer gives probabilistic systems a governed
  substrate for reasoning and correspondence.

The profile builds inspectable, versioned, machine-reasonable domain truth
(P3, P6). §5 states each core claim in full.

### Manifesto

The manifesto states the commitments the profile holds itself to (P1, P3,
P6; rc.2 #manifesto):

- observe source systems before attempting to standardise them;
- comprehend enacted semantics before flattening them into schemas;
- treat domains as bounded functional surfaces, not as loose
  vocabularies;
- treat data as evidence of function, never as meaning in isolation;
- recover the objects, boundaries, states, transitions, treatments and
  reference artifacts that experts already reason with implicitly;
- materialise that understanding into governed world-model artifacts
  rather than leaving it trapped in people, pipelines or detached
  documentation;
- build the world model to give rich conceptual knowledge of
  institutional systems, not merely better metadata about them;
- use that semantic substrate to tether AI or other probabilistic
  reasoning into bounded domain reality before asking it to align, compare
  or interpret across domains;
- use fully qualified naming to remove preventable aliasing before arguing
  about deeper semantic transformation;
- model cross-domain change as treatment, covariance and adjoint
  interpretation, not as naked field mapping;
- publish immutable semantic cuts and supersede them explicitly rather
  than silently rewriting truth in place;
- compose local fragments into higher-order views without erasing local
  authority;
- let probabilistic correspondence live in a governed epistemic overlay,
  then compile accepted understanding into deterministic execution where
  delivery requires real volume;
- treat projections as conveniences derived from the semantic kernel,
  never as co-equal constitutional truth;
- allow the world model to become generative only after it is
  descriptively grounded.

If these claims do not hold, the work is not genuinely
world-model-driven (P6).

## 3. What a world model is

A world model is a governed semantic layer built over existing source
systems. It is descriptive before it is generative (P3, P6;
rc.2 #what-a-world-model-is). Its primary semantic unit is the published
entity cut (§6 Units). Generic references to a "world-model object" are
shorthand for this unit unless the text explicitly names a looser
candidate or intermediate surface. A world model should make explicit:

- what objects are treated as real in a bounded context;
- what boundaries make those objects coherent;
- what state and transition surfaces matter;
- what evidence supports those claims;
- what value lists or classifications govern interpretation;
- how adjacent domains reinterpret the objects;
- what co-varies across those domains;
- what is preserved, changed, aggregated, delayed or lost.

A world model is not a replacement system of record; a central monolith
that erases local semantics; a generic catalog entry; a copied data
warehouse; or a schema registry pretending to be comprehension (P6).

### Objectives

The profile should produce world models that are bounded, inspectable,
challengeable, evidence-backed, versioned, composable,
projection-capable and machine-reasonable (P2; rc.2 #method-objectives).
If the profile is working, it should become possible to answer:

- what object is this record evidence of;
- what boundary defines that object;
- what source evidence supports the claim;
- which source is locally authoritative;
- what treatment changed the meaning downstream;
- what relationship exists between two differently treated objects;
- what value list or classification was in force at the time;
- why a downstream figure is or is not explainable from upstream reality.

### Evidence hierarchy

The profile works from the strongest available explanatory surfaces
(P6; rc.2 #evidence-hierarchy). Preferred hierarchy:

1. realised system behaviour and emitted records or events;
2. source code, rules and configuration when available;
3. interfaces and schemas;
4. large-scale observational data evidence;
5. documentation, catalogs and operator testimony.

Code is a high-fidelity explanatory surface when available. Realised
behaviour remains the broader truth surface because some systems are
proprietary, partially visible, or only intelligible through their
outputs and effects. The profile therefore never depends on code
availability as a prerequisite for comprehension (P6, P0).

### Overlay publication

A published Markov-object overlay makes visible, at minimum (P6, P3;
rc.2 #6-overlay-publication):

- representation space: the declared space and attribute basis the
  direction is expressed in, versioned so a later re-fit is detectable;
- identity direction: the projection (a representation-space vector or
  equivalent geometric description);
- attribute evidence: core and coat, traced to ledger entries;
- verification record: treatments used, outcomes observed, held-out
  contexts evaluated;
- boundary characterisation: the projection support on which identity was
  preserved under treatment;
- null-peer comparison: what similar things were tested, what was
  accepted, what was rejected;
- effective coordinate: α along the direction for this instance, where
  applicable.

An overlay is an immutable projection. Revising the identity direction,
because new contexts, new evidence or a re-fitted representation space
became available, produces a new overlay that supersedes the prior one.
A change of representation space changes every direction expressed in it
and is therefore a supersession even when no ledger entry changed. Prior
overlays are never mutated in place (§8 Publication law) (P4b, P3).

### Storage shape

The stored overlay is not a row in a table and not a membership list. It
is a package attached to its entity cut (P4b, P6;
rc.2 #7-storage-shape):

- the declared representation space and its version;
- the geometric object (identity direction and projection support);
- the distributed evidence (pointers into the attribute ledger);
- the verification trace (treatments, contexts, outcomes);
- the provenance and authority record (who accepted, on what assurance
  basis);
- the supersession record (which overlay this replaces, if any).

A storage layout that loses any of these surfaces is not a lawful
Markov-object storage layout. Query planes and projection surfaces
derived from stored cuts are downstream conveniences and do not
substitute for this storage shape (P6, P4b).

### Output

If the profile is working, the result is not just better metadata (P2;
rc.2 #method-output). The result is:

- published domain artifacts as the durable semantic publication units;
- composed world models built from those published artifacts;
- published entity cuts as the primary semantic units;
- inspectable Markov-object overlays where a representation space is
  selected;
- explicit treatment and covariance semantics;
- epistemic correspondence overlays grounded in the same semantic
  substrate;
- temporal reference artifacts for changing domain enumerations;
- familiar projections emitted from the same semantic substrate;
- deterministic compiled execution artifacts where the line must move
  real volume safely;
- a basis for AI reasoning over real institutional meaning.

## 5. Bad prose

Bad prose on these surfaces (P6, P1; rc.2 #bad-prose):

- implies conclusions instead of grounding them;
- repeats one claim in different words;
- mixes current reality with target intent without labelling the
  difference;
- explains basic concepts to an expert audience; or
- turns straightforward instructions into brand language.

